// The road the buildings stand along and the vehicles stand on.
//
//     import 'roads' { Road, Curb, Pavement, Junction, CurbCorner, Crossing }
//     import 'roads' { Crossroads, Roundabout, CurbArc, CurbRing }
//
//     object Road(80, 7)
//     object Curb(80)     { translate Z 4.1 }
//     object Pavement(80, 3) { translate Z 5.8 }
//
// | `Road`     | A carriageway: cambered, worn where the wheels run, with a line down the middle. |
// | `Curb`     | A curb run and the channel beside it, for the edge of a carriageway. |
// | `Pavement` | A footway in slabs, for behind the curb. |
// | `Junction`   | The mouth where a side road opens off a main one. |
// | `CurbCorner` | A quarter circle of curb, for the corner where two roads meet. |
// | `Crossing`   | A zebra, laid on a carriageway of the same width. |
// | `Crossroads` | Where two roads cross, with a stop line across each side arm. |
// | `Roundabout` | An island with a carriageway round it. |
// | `CurbArc`    | A curb along part of a circle, for where a ring has to be broken. |
// | `CurbRing`   | A whole circle of curb, for a roundabout's island. |
// | `ParkingLot` | A rectangle of asphalt with bays marked on it. |
//
// **The first two numbers are a length and a width**, because a road is the one thing in these
// libraries that genuinely has two sizes and no sensible ratio between them.  The two after them mean
// what they always mean, **what time of year** and **which one of that kind**.
//
// **These run along X**, so a road runs left to right in a camera looking the usual way -- the same
// direction the vehicles drive and the trains run, and for the same reason.
//
// **The road surface is y = 0** and everything else hangs below it, which is where `vehicles` puts the
// bottom of its tires.  So a car dropped on a road at the same origin stands on it, with nothing to work
// out.  It does mean a `plane` at y = 0 is the wrong ground for a street: put the verge a little lower,
// around -0.12, and let the curb stand out of it.
//
// -- Which one of that kind ------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The variant picks the surface, and here that is a bigger choice than a color:
//
// | `0` | New asphalt: nearly black, tight, the aggregate still buried in the binder. |
// | `1` | Worn asphalt: grayed by the sun, the aggregate showing, and cracked. |
// | `2` | Concrete: pale, and jointed rather than continuous. |
//
// -- What makes a slab into a road -----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The other libraries each ask this.  A road is the flattest thing in any of these scenes, so it has
// the least to work with and the answers are correspondingly small.
//
// **Camber.**  A road is crowned so the water runs off it, by about one part in forty.  That is far too
// little to see directly and it is most of what stops a carriageway reading as a sheet of paper: the
// crown catches the light differently from the channels, so a road gets a long soft highlight down its
// middle instead of one flat tone.  It is built here as strips at descending heights rather than as a
// curve, because a facet across half a meter at a fortieth of a gradient is not a facet anyone will see.
//
// **Wheel tracks.**  Traffic polishes two bands in each lane and leaves everything else matte -- the
// crown, the lane middles and the channels.  This is the single strongest cue that a road is *used*, and
// it costs nothing but choosing a material per strip.  It wants to be a *small* difference over a wide
// band: the first attempt gave the tracks three times the surround's specular, and what that reads as is
// two stripes painted down the road rather than wear.  The tracks sit where a car's wheels actually run:
// a lane centered at half the half-width, wheels three quarters of a meter either side of that.
//
// **Dirt at the edges.**  Nothing sweeps the last half meter against a curb, so it silts up.  A road
// that is the same color edge to edge reads as newly laid whatever its surface material says.
//
// **Asphalt is much darker than it looks.**  New asphalt returns about a twentieth of the light that
// falls on it, which is darker than almost anyone mixes it -- and the rock library's lesson applies
// exactly: a gray mixed too bright stops reading as a surface and starts reading as pale plastic.
//
// -- What is here for the library and not for you --------------------------------------------------
//
// Everything beginning with `Road` is the machinery, carrying the prefix for the reason the other
// libraries give: a material is looked up where it is *used*, so these arrive whether you asked or not.

// -- What a road is made of ------------------------------------------------------------------------

// New asphalt.  The speckle is the aggregate, and it is fine and low-contrast because in a new surface
// the stone is still buried -- what you see is binder with a hint of grain under it.
RoadNewAsphalt = material {
    pigment granite with seed 13 {
        [0.06, color [0.038, 0.038, 0.042],
         0.16, color [0.048, 0.048, 0.053],
         0.22, color [0.056, 0.056, 0.062],
         0.30, color [0.066, 0.066, 0.072],
         0.41, color [0.080, 0.080, 0.087]]
        scale 0.05
    }
    specular 0.10  shininess 22  ambient 0.05  brilliance 1.2
}

// The same, polished by tires: no lighter, but glossier, which is what a wheel track actually is.
RoadNewTrack = material {
    pigment granite with seed 13 {
        [0.06, color [0.034, 0.034, 0.038],
         0.16, color [0.042, 0.042, 0.047],
         0.22, color [0.049, 0.049, 0.055],
         0.30, color [0.058, 0.058, 0.065],
         0.41, color [0.070, 0.070, 0.078]]
        scale 0.05
    }
    specular 0.16  shininess 40  reflective 0.03  ambient 0.05  brilliance 1.2
}

// Worn asphalt, grayed by the sun with the aggregate showing through -- and cracked.
//
// **The cracks are the rare end of `crackle`, and knowing where that end actually is matters twice.**
// Crackle climbs from nought at its seed points towards the cell walls, so a map that darkens only near
// the top paints *only* the walls: a sparse net of thin dark lines over an even surface, which is what
// cracked asphalt looks like.  Spread the same map evenly over nought to one and the whole road turns
// into dark crazy paving.
//
// But the top is much lower than it looks.  Measured over sixty thousand points crackle's 95th
// percentile is **0.508** and its 99th is **0.666** -- so the first version of this, with its dark tones
// at 0.68 and 0.82, put them *past the end of the distribution* and rendered a road with no cracks in it
// at all.  Wanting cracks to be rare is right; putting them beyond reach is not the way to get it.  The
// dark end starts at 0.46 here -- about the 91st percentile, so roughly a twelfth of the surface.  Any
// higher and it picks out only crackle's *vertices*, where three cells meet, and renders as scattered
// dots rather than lines; 0.56 did exactly that.  A band this wide catches whole walls -- and the cells are 20 cm, not
// the 55 cm they started at, because the top few per cent of crackle is a *ring* around every wall
// rather than a line: at 55 cm those rings render as dark blobs, and only at 20 cm do they read as
// cracking.  The turbulence came down with them, since smearing a ring only makes it a wider ring.
RoadWornAsphalt = material {
    pigment crackle with seed 21 {
        turbulence 0.12
        [0.04, color [0.152, 0.150, 0.146],
         0.24, color [0.163, 0.161, 0.156],
         0.38, color [0.150, 0.148, 0.144],
         0.46, color [0.114, 0.112, 0.110],
         0.60, color [0.052, 0.051, 0.050]]
        scale 0.20
    }
    specular 0.06  shininess 14  ambient 0.055  brilliance 1.25
}

RoadWornTrack = material {
    pigment crackle with seed 21 {
        turbulence 0.12
        [0.04, color [0.130, 0.129, 0.126],
         0.24, color [0.140, 0.139, 0.136],
         0.38, color [0.128, 0.127, 0.124],
         0.46, color [0.098, 0.097, 0.095],
         0.60, color [0.046, 0.045, 0.044]]
        scale 0.20
    }
    specular 0.13  shininess 34  reflective 0.02  ambient 0.055  brilliance 1.25
}

// Concrete: pale, and the aggregate in it is coarser than asphalt's because there is no binder hiding it.
RoadConcrete = material {
    pigment granite with seed 29 {
        [0.06, color [0.225, 0.223, 0.212],
         0.16, color [0.262, 0.259, 0.246],
         0.22, color [0.288, 0.285, 0.271],
         0.30, color [0.318, 0.314, 0.298],
         0.41, color [0.355, 0.351, 0.333]]
        scale 0.09
    }
    specular 0.05  shininess 12  ambient 0.06  brilliance 1.3
}

RoadConcreteTrack = material {
    pigment granite with seed 29 {
        [0.06, color [0.195, 0.193, 0.184],
         0.16, color [0.228, 0.225, 0.214],
         0.22, color [0.250, 0.247, 0.235],
         0.30, color [0.276, 0.273, 0.259],
         0.41, color [0.308, 0.305, 0.289]]
        scale 0.09
    }
    specular 0.12  shininess 30  reflective 0.02  ambient 0.06  brilliance 1.3
}

// The silt against a curb: grit, dust and whatever the last rain left.  Browner than the road, always.
RoadEdgeDirt = material {
    pigment granite with seed 37 {
        turbulence 0.2
        [0.08, color [0.072, 0.066, 0.056],
         0.20, color [0.096, 0.088, 0.073],
         0.30, color [0.122, 0.111, 0.092],
         0.42, color [0.150, 0.136, 0.112]]
        scale 0.22
    }
    specular 0.03  shininess 9  ambient 0.055  brilliance 1.3
}

// Curbstone and paving: the same cast concrete, a shade apart so the two do not merge into one gray mass.
RoadCurbStone = material {
    pigment granite with seed 43 {
        [0.06, color [0.235, 0.232, 0.222],
         0.16, color [0.276, 0.272, 0.259],
         0.22, color [0.302, 0.298, 0.284],
         0.30, color [0.334, 0.329, 0.313],
         0.41, color [0.372, 0.367, 0.349]]
        scale 0.10
    }
    specular 0.06  shininess 16  ambient 0.06  brilliance 1.3
}

RoadSlab = material {
    pigment granite with seed 47 {
        [0.06, color [0.205, 0.203, 0.196],
         0.16, color [0.242, 0.239, 0.230],
         0.22, color [0.266, 0.263, 0.253],
         0.30, color [0.295, 0.291, 0.280],
         0.41, color [0.330, 0.326, 0.313]]
        scale 0.13
    }
    specular 0.05  shininess 14  ambient 0.06  brilliance 1.3
}

// Road paint is never white.  It is laid white, and then traffic and weather take it to a dirty cream
// within a season -- and a marking mixed at full white is the one thing on a road that will not sit
// down into the picture.
RoadPaint = material {
    pigment [0.545, 0.535, 0.495]  specular 0.10  shininess 20  ambient 0.07
}

// The build-up under the wearing course.  Plain on purpose: a *scaled* cube stretches whatever pattern
// it wears, and this one is thirty times longer than it is wide.
RoadBase = material {
    pigment [0.042, 0.042, 0.046]  specular 0.03  shininess 9  ambient 0.05
}

RoadSnow = material {
    pigment [0.90, 0.92, 0.96]  specular 0.24  shininess 50  ambient 0.10
}

// -- Choosing a surface ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

primitive RoadPlainOf(variant) -> material {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 0 { return material RoadNewAsphalt }
        case 1 { return material RoadWornAsphalt }
        default { return material RoadConcrete }
    }
}

primitive RoadTrackOf(variant) -> material {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 0 { return material RoadNewTrack }
        case 1 { return material RoadWornTrack }
        default { return material RoadConcreteTrack }
    }
}

// Whether a strip of the carriageway is a wheel track.
//
// `across` is nought at the crown and one at the channel.  A lane sits centered on half of that, and a
// car's wheels run about three quarters of a meter either side of the lane's middle -- so on a
// seven-meter road the tracks land near 0.29 and 0.71, which is a band either side of 0.5.  Everything
// else stays matte: the crown, the middle of each lane, and the channel.
primitive RoadSurfaceAt(variant, across) -> material {
    if (abs(across - 0.5) > 0.08 and abs(across - 0.5) < 0.35) {
        return material RoadTrackOf(variant)
    }
    else {
        return material RoadPlainOf(variant)
    }
}

// -- Snow ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Winter on a road is not winter on a roof: **the traffic clears the tracks and nothing clears the rest**.
// So snow lies along the crown, where no wheel runs, and banks up in the channels where it is thrown --
// and a road under snow with a clean unbroken white surface is a road nobody has driven since it fell.
primitive RoadWinter(season, length, width) -> group {
    switch (season) {
        case 'winter' {
            return group {
                cube {
                    material RoadSnow
                    scale [length * 0.5, 0.008, width * 0.070]
                    translate Y 0.006
                }
                cube {
                    material RoadSnow
                    scale [length * 0.5, 0.010, width * 0.085]
                    translate [0, -width * 0.0116, width * 0.415]
                }
                cube {
                    material RoadSnow
                    scale [length * 0.5, 0.010, width * 0.085]
                    translate [0, -width * 0.0116, -width * 0.415]
                }
            }
        }
        default { return group { } }
    }
}

// -- Joints ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Concrete is laid in bays and asphalt is not, so a concrete carriageway carries a joint across it every
// few meters and an asphalt one carries none.
//
// **A joint is a gap, not a dark line laid on top.**  The first attempt was a thin dark cube across the
// road at half the camber's depth, and it rendered as almost nothing: a single cube is flat and the road
// is crowned, so the joint sat *under* the wearing course across most of the width and poked out only
// near the channels, giving one odd diagonal mark. Raising it would have made it a bar standing proud of
// the crown instead. What a joint actually is, is a place where the surface stops -- so the wearing
// course is laid in bays with a gap between them, and the dark build-up underneath shows through.
//
// Asphalt gets one bay the length of the road and a gap of nothing, so the same loop serves both and
// pays nothing for the surface it does not need.
//
// **A joint is a close-up detail and there is no fixing that.**  Three centimeters is about what a
// sealed expansion joint measures, and on a road seen from fifteen meters that is a fifth of a pixel --
// which point sampling misses altogether rather than dimming. Widening it enough to survive at that
// distance would mean a joint eight centimeters across, which is not a joint. So it reads from a car's
// length away and not from across a field, which is also true of the real thing.
function RoadBaysOf(variant, length) -> number {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 2 { return max(1, floor(length / 4.5)) }
        default { return 1 }
    }
}

function RoadJointOf(variant) -> number {
    switch (mod(floor(variant), 3)) {
        case 2 { return 0.03 }
        default { return 0 }
    }
}

// -- A carriageway ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The carriageway itself -- body, wearing course and the silt in the channels -- with no markings on it.
// `Road` adds those; a `Junction` is this and nothing else, because markings stop at a junction.
primitive RoadDeck(length, width, variant) -> group {
    half = width * 0.5
    crown = half * 0.025
    strips = floor(width / 0.5)
    step = width / strips
    bays = RoadBaysOf(variant, length)
    bayLength = length / bays
    joint = RoadJointOf(variant)

    return group {
        // The build-up, with its top at the channel so nothing pokes through the wearing course above.
        cube {
            material RoadBase
            scale [length * 0.5, 0.07, half]
            translate Y -crown - 0.07
        }

        // **The wearing course is `parallelogram`s and not cubes, and that is not a saving -- it is the
        // only way the pattern comes out right.**  A pattern is worked out in the surface's own space, so
        // a strip built as a cube scaled [30, 0.06, 0.25] stretches its granite thirty times along the
        // road and squashes it to a quarter across: the first version of this rendered a set of long
        // longitudinal streaks and not one crack anywhere.  A parallelogram is given a corner and two
        // edges and carries no transform at all, so its pattern is in world coordinates -- which also
        // makes the cracks run *continuously across the strip seams* instead of each strip holding its
        // own stretched copy of the same patch.
        for strip in [0, strips - 1] {
            for bay in [0, bays - 1] {
                // **Each strip is tilted to the camber rather than laid flat at its own height**, so
                // consecutive strips meet exactly along their shared edge and the surface is continuous.
                // Flat strips leave a vertical step at every seam, and neither way of hiding that works:
                // butting them shows the dark build-up through the step, and overlapping them by a
                // centimeter makes the higher strip's edge a lip that casts a thin shadow down the road.
                // A parallelogram takes two edge *vectors*, so giving the second one a Y component costs
                // nothing and removes the step instead of covering it.
                parallelogram {
                    material RoadSurfaceAt(variant, abs((strip + 0.5) * step - half) / half)
                    at [bay * bayLength - length * 0.5,
                        -crown * (abs(strip * step - half) / half)
                               * (abs(strip * step - half) / half),
                        strip * step - half]
                    sides [bayLength - joint, 0, 0],
                          [0,
                           -crown * (abs((strip + 1) * step - half) / half)
                                  * (abs((strip + 1) * step - half) / half)
                           + crown * (abs(strip * step - half) / half)
                                   * (abs(strip * step - half) / half),
                           step]
                }
            }
        }

        // The silt against each curb, laid on the channel rather than instead of it.
        for side in [0, 1] {
            parallelogram {
                material RoadEdgeDirt
                at [-length * 0.5, -crown * 0.90 + 0.002,
                    (half - width * 0.055) * (1 - side * 2) - width * 0.055]
                sides [length, 0, 0], [0, 0, width * 0.110]
            }
        }
    }
}

primitive Road(length, width, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    half = width * 0.5
    crown = half * 0.025

    return group {
        object RoadDeck(length, width, variant)

        // A dashed line down the middle, and a solid one in each channel.  The dashes are two meters in
        // six, which is close enough to what a road actually carries that the eye stops counting.
        for dash in [0, floor(length / 6) - 1] {
            cube {
                material RoadPaint
                scale [1.0, 0.003, 0.055]
                translate [(dash + 0.5) * 6 - length * 0.5, 0.002, 0]
            }
        }
        for side in [0, 1] {
            cube {
                material RoadPaint
                scale [length * 0.5, 0.003, 0.055]
                translate [0, -crown * 0.60 + 0.002,
                           (half - width * 0.10) * (1 - side * 2)]
            }
        }

        object RoadWinter(season, length, width)
    }
}

// -- A junction ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Where a side road opens off a main one.  `width` is the main road's width and `opening` is how far
// along it the mouth reaches -- which is the side road's width plus its two corner radii.
//
// **A junction is a piece of main road, not a shape of its own.**  The main road's camber runs straight
// through and the side road ramps up to meet it, which is what a real side turning does; the alternative
// is to invent a surface that drains both ways at once, and then neither road's channel lines up with
// it. So this is the same deck with the markings left off, and a scene lays road either side of it:
//
//     object Road(30, 7)     { translate X -19 }
//     object Junction(7, 8)
//     object Road(30, 7)     { translate X  19 }
//
// Markings stop at a junction, which is why this carries none. The side road's own `Road` provides its.
primitive Junction(width, opening, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    return group {
        object RoadDeck(opening, width, variant)
        object RoadWinter(season, opening, width)
    }
}

// -- A curb ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Laid along X with its face at z = 0, so it goes at the edge of a carriageway: `translate Z half`.
primitive Curb(length, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    return group {
        // A plain body for the buried part and the cut ends, and the two faces that show as
        // parallelograms so their stone is in world space -- see the note in `Road`.
        cube {
            material RoadBase
            scale [length * 0.5, 0.165, 0.075]
            translate [0, 0.125 - 0.165, 0.075]
        }
        parallelogram {
            material RoadCurbStone
            at [-length * 0.5, 0.1252, 0]
            sides [length, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0.15]
        }
        parallelogram {
            material RoadCurbStone
            at [-length * 0.5, 0.125, -0.0002]
            sides [length, 0, 0], [0, -0.26, 0]
        }
        object RoadWinter(season, length, 0.001)
    }
}

// -- A pavement ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A footway in real slabs rather than a slab with joints painted on it, because the joints are what a
// pavement *is* -- and a joint with a little depth in it catches a shadow that a painted line cannot.
primitive Pavement(length, width, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    along = floor(length / 1.2)
    across = floor(width / 1.2)
    stepAlong = length / along
    stepAcross = width / across

    return group {
        // The bed the slabs sit on, so the joints between them are dark rather than transparent.
        cube {
            material RoadEdgeDirt
            scale [length * 0.5, 0.06, width * 0.5]
            translate Y 0.125 - 0.06 - 0.045
        }
        for row in [0, along - 1] {
            for column in [0, across - 1] {
                cube {
                    material RoadSlab
                    scale [stepAlong * 0.5 - 0.012, 0.024, stepAcross * 0.5 - 0.012]
                    translate [(row + 0.5) * stepAlong - length * 0.5,
                               0.125 - 0.024,
                               (column + 0.5) * stepAcross - width * 0.5]
                }
            }
        }

        object RoadWinter(season, length, width * 0.42)
    }
}

// -- A parking lot ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A rectangle of asphalt with bays marked on it: `width` across, `depth` back, two rows of bays against
// the long edges with an aisle between them.  Flat and at y = 0, like everything else here, so a car set
// down on it stands on it.
//
// The surface is a single `parallelogram` for the reason the carriageway's strips are: it carries no
// transform, so its asphalt is in world coordinates instead of being stretched across the lot.
//
// Bays are two and a half meters wide and five deep, which is what a bay is; the count comes from the
// width rather than the other way about, so a lot is whatever size the scene needs and the bays divide
// it evenly.
primitive ParkingLot(width, depth, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    bays = max(1, floor(width / 2.5))
    bayWidth = width / bays
    bayDepth = min(5, depth * 0.42)

    return group {
        cube {
            material RoadBase
            scale [width * 0.5, 0.07, depth * 0.5]
            translate Y -0.07
        }
        parallelogram {
            material RoadPlainOf(variant)
            at [-width * 0.5, 0, -depth * 0.5]
            sides [width, 0, 0], [0, 0, depth]
        }
        // The silt along the edges, where nothing sweeps -- the same thing the carriageway's channels get.
        for side in [0, 1] {
            parallelogram {
                material RoadEdgeDirt
                at [-width * 0.5, 0.002, (depth * 0.5 - 0.4) * (1 - side * 2) - 0.4]
                sides [width, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0.8]
            }
        }
        // The bay lines: one more line than there are bays, since a row of n bays has n + 1 sides.
        for row in [0, 1] {
            for line in [0, bays] {
                cube {
                    material RoadPaint
                    scale [0.055, 0.003, bayDepth * 0.5]
                    translate [line * bayWidth - width * 0.5, 0.003,
                               (depth * 0.5 - bayDepth * 0.5) * (1 - row * 2)]
                }
            }
        }

        object RoadWinter(season, width, depth)
    }
}

// -- An arc of curb --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A curb along part of a circle: `from` is where it starts, in degrees measured from +X towards +Z, and
// `sweep` is how far round it goes.  `CurbCorner` is the ninety-degree case of this and `CurbRing` the
// whole circle; this is what you want wherever the circle is *broken*, which round a roundabout is at
// every entry.
primitive CurbArc(radius, from, sweep, facing, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    // How many segments it takes to keep the chords within about a centimeter of the true circle.  The
    // count goes as the **square root** of the radius, not the radius: the gap a chord leaves is
    // `r * (1 - cos(turn / 2))`, so holding it fixed makes the segment angle scale as one over root r.
    // A rule linear in the radius therefore over-segments a big arc and under-segments a small one --
    // and a small one is where it shows, since a tight corner is what a camera gets close to.
    segments = max(3, floor(sweep * sqrt(radius) / 14))
    turn = sweep / segments
    chord = 2 * radius * sin((turn * 0.5) degrees)

    return group {
        for segment in [0, segments - 1] {
            object Curb(chord * 1.04, season, variant) {
                rotate Y ((facing * 2 - 1) * 90 - from - (segment + 0.5) * turn)
                translate [radius * cos((from + (segment + 0.5) * turn) degrees),
                           0,
                           radius * sin((from + (segment + 0.5) * turn) degrees)]
            }
        }
    }
}

// -- A rounded corner ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A quarter circle of curb, for the corner where two roads meet.  It is what makes a junction read: two
// straight curb runs mitred at a right angle look like a drawing, and a radius looks like a street.  The
// arc runs from `+X` round to `+Z` with its center of curvature at the origin, so the pavement is on the
// inside of it and the road outside -- which is the way round a street corner is.
//
// **This is exactly `CurbArc` over ninety degrees, and is written as a call to it** rather than as a
// second loop.  It was a second loop first, and the two drifted immediately -- different segment counts,
// and only one of them overlapping its chords -- while the comment here claimed they were the same thing.
// A test that renders both and compares them pixel for pixel caught that on its first run.  A claim like
// that is better made true by construction than checked.
//
// **Its origin is the center of curvature, not the corner**, and that is the thing to get right.  For a
// pavement corner at `(cx, cz)` with the road on the `+X` and `+Z` sides of it, the center sits a radius
// *diagonally inside* the pavement at `(cx - r, cz - r)`, and the arc then meets the two straight runs
// at `(cx - r, cz)` and `(cx, cz - r)` -- so those runs have to stop a radius short of the corner.  Put
// the origin on the corner itself and the arc swings away into the pavement, leaving a gap at both ends;
// that is what the first version of the junction scene did.
//
// `cos` and `sin` take radians while every transform here is written in degrees, and the language settles
// that with a postfix `degrees` operator (`°` also works) -- so the angle stays in degrees throughout and
// is marked at the point it reaches the trigonometry.  It binds to its immediate operand, so a computed
// angle needs its own parentheses: `cos((a * b) degrees)`, not `cos(a * b degrees)`.
//
// The face has to end up pointing away from the center, and working out which turn does that is worth
// spelling out: `Curb` runs along X with its exposed face looking towards -Z, and under `rotate Y θ`
// local -Z lands on `(-sin θ, 0, -cos θ)`. Setting that equal to the outward normal `(cos a, 0, sin a)`
// gives `θ = -90 - a`, which is what the loop uses.
primitive CurbCorner(radius, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    return group {
        object CurbArc(radius, 0, 90, 0, season, variant)
    }
}

// -- A crossing ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A zebra, laid on a carriageway of the same width.  `depth` is how far along the road it reaches, which
// is how wide the crossing is to walk over.
//
// **The bars run along the road, not across it**, which surprises people who have not looked lately: you
// walk across a zebra between its stripes, and the stripes are parallel to the traffic. That turns out
// to be the same geometry the carriageway is already built from -- a bar is one strip of it painted -- so
// each bar picks up the camber for free by being tilted exactly as the strip beneath it is.
primitive Crossing(width, depth, season = 'summer') -> group {
    half = width * 0.5
    crown = half * 0.025
    strips = floor(width / 0.5)
    step = width / strips

    return group {
        for strip in [0, strips - 1] {
            // Every other strip, so the bars and the gaps come out the same width -- which on a road
            // built in half-meter strips is the half meter a real zebra uses.
            if (mod(strip, 2) == 0) {
                parallelogram {
                    material RoadPaint
                    at [-depth * 0.5,
                        -crown * (abs(strip * step - half) / half)
                               * (abs(strip * step - half) / half) + 0.003,
                        strip * step - half]
                    sides [depth, 0, 0],
                          [0,
                           -crown * (abs((strip + 1) * step - half) / half)
                                  * (abs((strip + 1) * step - half) / half)
                           + crown * (abs(strip * step - half) / half)
                                   * (abs(strip * step - half) / half),
                           step]
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// -- A ring of curb --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A full circle of curb, which a roundabout needs twice: once round the island with its face outward,
// and once round the outside of the carriageway with its face inward.
//
// This is `CurbArc` over the whole 360, and is a call to it for the reason `CurbCorner` is: a second copy
// of the loop drifts from the first, and did.
primitive CurbRing(radius, facing, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    return group {
        object CurbArc(radius, 0, 360, facing, season, variant)
    }
}

// -- A four-way intersection -----------------------------------------------------------------------

// Where two roads cross.  Like `Junction`, this is a piece of the main road rather than a shape of its
// own: the main road's camber runs through it and both side arms ramp up to meet it.  What it adds over
// a `Junction` is the pair of **stop lines**, one across each side arm, and those are most of what makes
// a crossing of two roads read as a junction rather than as a hole where the markings stop.
//
//     object Road(30, 7)              { translate X -19.5 }
//     object Crossroads(7, 6, 1.5)
//     object Road(30, 7)              { translate X  19.5 }
//     object Road(30, 6) { rotate Y 90  translate Z -19.5 }
//     object Road(30, 6) { rotate Y 90  translate Z  19.5 }
//
// The deck reaches a corner radius past the side road on each side, so there is carriageway under the
// rounded corners; the four `CurbCorner`s go at the same radius, each centered a radius diagonally
// inside its own pavement corner.
//
// **Each side arm has to reach the main road's own edge**, at `mainWidth / 2`, and not stop where the
// corner radius begins.  Stopping at the radius leaves a strip of bare ground as wide as that radius
// running right across the mouth, which is what the first junction scene here did.
primitive Crossroads(mainWidth, sideWidth, radius, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    half = mainWidth * 0.5
    crown = half * 0.025
    opening = sideWidth + radius * 2

    return group {
        object RoadDeck(opening, mainWidth, variant)

        // A stop line across each side arm, laid just outside the mouth so it reads as belonging to the
        // arm rather than to the crossing.  Solid, and wider than a lane line, which is what a stop line
        // is: it is the one marking a driver is meant to stop *at* rather than follow.
        for arm in [0, 1] {
            cube {
                material RoadPaint
                scale [sideWidth * 0.5, 0.003, 0.10]
                translate [0, -crown * 0.35 + 0.002, (half + 0.10) * (1 - arm * 2)]
            }
        }

        // **The four corner fillets**, without which there is bare ground at every corner of the
        // junction.  The side arms reach the main road's edge and no further, so beyond that edge the
        // only thing paved is whatever lies *outside* the curb arc -- and that is a square of side
        // `radius` with a quarter circle bitten out of it, which is a `difference` of a cube and a
        // cylinder.  It is not a sliver: against a 1.5 m radius the gap is nearly a meter wide where it
        // meets the main road, narrowing to nothing at the far end of the arc.
        //
        // Flat, at about the main road's channel: these are a meter and a half across, and the two or
        // three centimeters they miss the camber by at their edges is not something a camera resolves.
        //
        // The shape is symmetric under a quarter turn, so one shape turned four ways serves -- but the
        // *placement* is not, since `sideWidth` and `mainWidth` are different numbers, so the two signs
        // are worked out per corner.  Turn 0 lands the shape in +X/+Z, turn 90 in +X/-Z, 180 in -X/-Z
        // and 270 in -X/+Z, which is what the two `floor`s below spell out.
        for corner in [0, 3] {
            difference {
                cube { scale [radius * 0.5, 0.03, radius * 0.5]
                       translate [radius * 0.5, 0, radius * 0.5] }
                cylinder { min Y -1  max Y 1
                           scale [radius, 0.5, radius]
                           translate [radius, 0, radius] }
                material RoadPlainOf(variant)
                rotate Y (corner * 90)
                translate [(1 - 2 * floor(corner * 0.5)) * sideWidth * 0.5,
                           -crown * 0.9,
                           (1 - 2 * floor(mod(corner + 1, 4) * 0.5)) * half]
            }
        }

        object RoadWinter(season, opening, mainWidth)
    }
}

// -- A roundabout ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// An island with a carriageway round it.  `radius` is to the island's curb face and `width` is how wide
// the circulating carriageway is, so the whole thing measures `radius + width` from the middle.
//
// **The carriageway is `disc`s, and that is the same trick the straight road plays with
// `parallelogram`s.**  A disc is given a center, a normal and a radius, and an `inner radius` makes it
// an annulus -- so it carries no transform, and its pattern is in world coordinates rather than
// stretched round the ring.  Three concentric annuli give the worn band where the traffic actually
// circulates, and because they are all flat and share their radii exactly they abut with no seam at all.
//
// **It is flat, and that is a deliberate simplification.**  A real roundabout has a crossfall, and there
// is no honest way to put one here: a disc is planar by definition, and stacking concentric rings at
// descending heights brings back exactly the vertical-step seams that the straight road had to be tilted
// to avoid -- and a disc, unlike a parallelogram, cannot be tilted without becoming an ellipse.  A
// crossfall over six meters is fifteen centimeters, which is worth less than a ring of dark seams.
//
// The island is paved rather than planted, because paving is something this library owns and grass is
// not.  A scene wanting a green one can lay `undergrowth`'s `Grass` over the top of it.
primitive Roundabout(radius, width, season = 'summer', variant = 1) -> group {
    return group {
        // The circulating carriageway, in three rings: the middle one is where the wheels go.
        disc {
            material RoadPlainOf(variant)
            center [0, 0, 0]  normal [0, 1, 0]
            radius radius + width * 0.28
            inner radius radius
        }
        disc {
            material RoadTrackOf(variant)
            center [0, 0, 0]  normal [0, 1, 0]
            radius radius + width * 0.72
            inner radius radius + width * 0.28
        }
        disc {
            material RoadPlainOf(variant)
            center [0, 0, 0]  normal [0, 1, 0]
            radius radius + width
            inner radius radius + width * 0.72
        }
        // The silt that collects against the outer curb, where nothing sweeps.
        disc {
            material RoadEdgeDirt
            center [0, 0.002, 0]  normal [0, 1, 0]
            radius radius + width
            inner radius radius + width * 0.93
        }
        // The build-up under it all.
        cylinder {
            material RoadBase
            min Y -1  max Y 1
            scale [radius + width, 0.035, radius + width]
            translate Y -0.035
        }

        // The island: a curb round it facing out, and paving inside that at the curb's own height.
        //
        // **The paving stops at the curb's inner edge, not past its face.**  An outward-facing curb has
        // its body on the *inside* -- that is what makes the face the outside of the island -- so paving
        // taken out to `radius + 0.15` covers the curb completely, which is how the first version of this
        // came out with no visible curb at all.  `radius - 0.15` meets the body's inner edge instead.
        object CurbRing(radius, 0, season, variant)
        disc {
            material RoadSlab
            center [0, 0.125, 0]  normal [0, 1, 0]
            radius radius - 0.15
        }
        // **There is no curb round the outside, and that is on purpose.**  A roundabout's outer curb is
        // broken at every entry, so a full ring lays a curb straight across each approach and walls the
        // roads off -- which is what the first version of this did.  Teaching this primitive about arms
        // would mean telling it how many there are, how wide, and at what angles, and it would still be
        // wrong for the roundabout whose arms are not evenly spaced.  So the scene lays `CurbArc`s
        // between its own entries, which is the same division of labor `Junction` uses:
        //
        //     object Roundabout(4.5, 6)
        //     for arm in [0, 3] {
        //         object CurbArc(10.5, 21 + arm * 90, 48, 1)
        //     }
        //
        // **And a straight road meeting a circle has to be pushed inside it.**  Ended at the outer
        // radius, a road touches the circle along its center line only, leaving a crescent of bare
        // ground at each corner -- 60 cm of it for a seven-meter road on a 10.5 m circle.  The end
        // belongs at `sqrt(outer * outer - halfWidth * halfWidth)`, where the road's two *corners* land
        // on the circle and its middle overlaps into the annulus.  The overlap is harmless: the annulus
        // is flat at nought and the road's camber falls away from it, so the annulus wins there.
    }
}
